<p>I would like to claim that this website is not a "vanity project".  But let's face it, it is.  My main purpose in creating this is to have a little corner of the web where I am able to write my own opinions (at length) and put them up here for all to see.  But this is not a blog.</p>
<p>I claim to see the world differently from other people.  If you've met me, you will have your own opinion about that.  A specific claim I make is to ask "why is that done that way?" all the time - and generally not accept any fluff answers.  To me, a blog is organized by time.  This may make sense: blogs took off in popularity in the US election campaign of 2001, where it really did feel like a "diary".  I want to write "blog length" articles, but I want to organise them thematically.</p>
<p>But I also want to challenge all sorts of other "sacred cows".  Well, actually, I think most of them are just assumptions people made, or defaults that happened, rather than anything anybody actually cared about.  And specifically, because I am a technology guy, I want to challenge the technology we use to build things.</p>
<p>Somewhere in the dawn of time (I was alive and programming at the time, but I didn't see the decision made), it was decided that all web pages would, by default, be infinite in size and scroll vertically.  Before you say, "well, obviously" I can tell you that "people" did not find this obvious when they first encountered it.  It was surprising and counter-intuitive, because we were used to programs with fixed-size screens and multiple pages.  Books definitely had multiple pages.  You will find on this site that content which is "too big" to fit in the space available is handled in different ways: it may scroll vertically or horizontally; it may be paged; it may be in a hierarchy.  I have done what I have done for my own reasons.</p>
<p>In the same vein, I was around when the decision was made to have lists on websites "go upwards" and I know why: in the early days of AJAX, it was all but impossible to even get anything working.  Wrangling with IE6 took up most of software developers' time.  So, if you could manage to get something back from a server, just "stick it at the top of the div" seemed easy and avoided scrolling.  But it sucks.  When I briefly used facebook in 2010 or so, the nested up-down-up thing of just reading a single page blew my mind.  I'm amazed more people don't complain about this, since everything from newsfeeds to twitter and bluesky work this way.  I'm not sure if it appears anywhere on this website, but I did figure out how to make all that work properly and attempted to package it.</p>
<p>Likewise, there is a tendency to use lots of words to describe things and to organise them in lists.  I have done both of these for the same reason everybody else does - it's easy and I'm lazy.  But I have tried to make the navigation of this site different by using more pictures to suggest meanings.</p>
<p>My wife describes the result as "arty".  I don't think she means it in a complimentary way.  Fair enough.  I am not a particularly artistic person, but inasmuch as I am, yes, it is here.  I want to be clear that I do <b>not</b> enjoy playing with Photoshop, but I do enjoy taking pictures with my camera.  I like many of those pictures, but I often don't feel they fully capture what was in my head.  Photoshop may be painful, but it does - particularly with the Oil Paint filter - help me realise what I had in mind when I took the photo.</p>
<p>So my claim that this site is not a "vanity project" is mainly wrapped up in this claim: I think that most of the technology that has been used to build the web sucks, and most of the people who have built most of the sites on the web have just gone along with that.  For the past decade or so, I have tried to consciously push against that, and everything that I have done here is either an experiment in that direction, or an attempt to use something I have experimented with.</p>
<p>It all started with my job search in 2017/18.  I didn't want to just print off and submit a boring CV that said "I'm amazing", just like everyone else.  More than that, I didn't want to say "I'm old" (which is all that a 12-page resume says, except possibly I change jobs a lot).  So I thought, what can I do?  You can see the result here in  <a href='https://hireme.gmmapowell.com'>the hireme section</a> (assuming it hasn't given in to software rot by the time you get there).  There are a number of things going on here, but what I really wanted to showcase was that I could write some fairly cunning JavaScript (as Baldrick would say), and you also had the chance to look over it and see what I considered acceptable JavaScript code.  Sadly, nobody took the bait and said "just send us your CV".  When I responded "there's a print button in your browser; try it", most people just simply didn't believe that anything useful would be printed off.  Only one person had the decency to be amazed that what came off <i>looked nothing like what was on the screen</i> and everything like a normal boring CV.  It still irritates me <i>to this day</i> that this is the standard of the industry I work in.  With CSS, <i>every</i> website should display properly on the screen and print properly on the printer.  This website should be the same (and hopefully will be one day).</p>
<p>The dynamic side of this website is all built using my own tools and programming language, FLAS.  It's one of the testbeds for that.  I'm still not sure how I feel about what I built, but it is certainly better than trying to handroll everything in JavaScript.  FLAS came about because I couldn't bear to deal with either vanilla JavaScript or with any kind of plumbing on top of that, and I wanted something much more dynamic, reactive and scalable.  Not to mention simpler.  I achieved <i>that</i>, but I'm still not sure I'm happy: so many things still seem so much more clunky on paper than they are in my head.</p>
<p>This site does not use REST per se, but it does use meaningful URLs.  You should be able to bookmark anything you want and you should be able to get back to it.  From time to time I will move things around, so the bookmarks may not always work, though.  Some articles have permalinks to them but I haven't done a good job of exposing that as yet.</p>
<p>So look around, have fun, and let me know what you think.  I should remind you that all the opinions on this site, along with all the experiments, are my own.</p>
<p>Oh, and there aren't any ads on this site and hopefully never will be, and I have certainly not put any cookies (tracking or otherwise) up here or anything else.  It's conceivable that at some point I will use <tt>LocalStorage</tt> to keep some state to help you navigate the site, but if I do I won't share it with the wider world.</p>
